<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Derby</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Derby is a city in the East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midlands">Midlands</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> on the River Derwent. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a> it was one of the Five Boroughs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danelaw">Danelaw</a>, taken by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelflaed">Æthelflæd</a> of Mercia in 917. In the 18th century the Lombe brothers' <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> mill made it an early centre of factory industry, part of the Derwent Valley Mills World Heritage Site, and it later became a base for railway and aero-engine manufacture.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/derby.jpg" alt="Derby" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: GR77 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Derby_Cathedral_at_night.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Jacobite risings in support of the exiled Stuarts were defeated in 1715 and in 1745–1746, when Charles Edward Stuart reached <b>Derby</b> before his army was destroyed at the Battle of Culloden.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Danelaw of eastern and northern England, Norse and English speakers lived side by side, and hundreds of place names ending in -by, “farm” or “village”, and -thorpe, “outlying settlement”, such as <b>Derby</b>, Grimsby, Whitby and Scunthorpe, mark their settlements.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-norse">Old Norse · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Overruling Charles Edward Stuart, his commanders began a retreat from <b>Derby</b>, about 125 miles from London.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-6">6 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>During the <b>Derby</b>, Emily Wilding Davison stepped onto the course and was knocked down by Anmer, George V's horse.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-4">4 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The victory encouraged the Jacobite invasion of England, which turned back at <b>Derby</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-21">21 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Aethelflaed of Mercia and Edward the Elder took <b>Derby</b>, Leicester, Nottingham and Stamford in 917 and 918.</blockquote><a href="/state/five-boroughs-of-the-danelaw">Five Boroughs of the Danelaw</a></li></ul></section>
